The Jacobian is the collection of all first-order partial derivatives of a vector-valued function $ f : \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}^m $. It is an $ m \times n $ matrix, where each entry $ J(i, j) $ is given by $ \frac{\partial f_i}{\partial x_j} $ [2]. 

For example, if we have a function $ f : \mathbb{R}^2 \to \mathbb{R}^2 $, the Jacobian matrix $ J $ is given by:

$$
J = \begin{bmatrix} 
\frac{\partial f_1}{\partial x_1} & \frac{\partial f_1}{\partial x_2} \\ 
\frac{\partial f_2}{\partial x_1} & \frac{\partial f_2}{\partial x_2} 
\end{bmatrix}
$$

This matrix represents the linear transformation that approximates the function $ f $ near a point. If the coordinate transformation is linear, the Jacobian is exact; otherwise, it approximates the nonlinear transformation locally with a linear one [1]. The absolute value of the determinant of the Jacobian, $ |\det(J)| $, gives the factor by which areas or volumes are scaled under the transformation [1].

